% Problem: Building a Lagrange Polynomial by Hand and Verifying It
% Name:
% Date:

clear; clc;

% a) Nested-loop Lagrange interpolation at xq = 3
x = [0 2 4];
y = [3 11 9];
xq = 3;


% b) Verify with polyfit/polyval


% c) Compare against interp1's default linear method


% d) Comment: why do (a) and (b) match, but not (c)?

solutionCode:
% a) Nested-loop Lagrange interpolation
x = [0 2 4];
y = [3 11 9];
xq = 3;

L_atQuery = 0;
for ii = 1:length(x)
    term = 1;
    for jj = 1:length(x)
        if jj ~= ii
            term = term * (xq - x(jj)) / (x(ii) - x(jj));
        end
    end
    L_atQuery = L_atQuery + y(ii) * term;
end
disp(L_atQuery)

% b) Verify with polyfit/polyval
p = polyfit(x, y, 2);
poly_atQuery = polyval(p, xq);
disp(poly_atQuery)
disp(abs(L_atQuery - poly_atQuery) < 1e-9)

% c) Compare against interp1
interp_atQuery = interp1(x, y, xq);
disp(interp_atQuery)

% d) poly_atQuery matches L_atQuery exactly because both methods construct
% the SAME unique degree-(n-1) polynomial that passes through all n given
% points -- there is only one such polynomial, so any correct method for
% finding it must agree. interp1's default method instead builds a
% piecewise-linear curve that only ever looks at the two nearest points
% bracketing the query (here, (2,11) and (4,9)), completely ignoring the
% point at x=0, which is why it returns a different value (10).

% a) Create the data
temp = [10 20 30 40 50 60];
velocity = [40 52 58 60 55 45];

% b) Exact degree-5 fit
poly5 = polyfit(temp, velocity, 5);
checkFit = polyval(poly5, temp);
disp(checkFit)   % should match velocity almost exactly

% c) Degree-1 fit and fine-grid maxima
poly1 = polyfit(temp, velocity, 1);
xf = linspace(10, 60, 200);
y5 = polyval(poly5, xf);
y1 = polyval(poly1, xf);
maxY5 = max(y5);
maxY1 = max(y1);
fprintf('maxY5 = %.4f, maxY1 = %.4f\n', maxY5, maxY1);

% d) Overshoot flag
overshoots = maxY5 > max(velocity);
disp(overshoots)

% e) Evaluate both fits at temp = 35
v5_35 = polyval(poly5, 35);
v1_35 = polyval(poly1, 35);
fprintf('poly5 at 35: %.4f, poly1 at 35: %.4f\n', v5_35, v1_35);
% poly5 (about 59.7) is more trustworthy here, since temp = 35 sits right
% in the middle of the data where poly5's exact fit and rise-then-fall
% shape closely tracks the nearby measured points (58 at 30, 60 at 40).
% poly1 (about 51.7) misses the peak entirely, since a single straight
% line can't represent a curve that rises and then falls.

% a) Define f and the exact value
f = @(x) x.^3;
a = 0; b = 2;
trueValue = b^4/4 - a^4/4;

% b) Loop over increasing n
ns = [4 8 16];
estimates = zeros(1,3);
errs = zeros(1,3);
for k = 1:length(ns)
    n = ns(k);
    h = (b - a) / n;
    x = a:h:b;
    estimates(k) = trapz(x, f(x));
    errs(k) = abs(estimates(k) - trueValue);
end

% c) Display errors and their ratios
disp(errs)
ratio1 = errs(1) / errs(2);
ratio2 = errs(2) / errs(3);
fprintf('ratio1 = %.4f, ratio2 = %.4f\n', ratio1, ratio2);

% d) Both ratios come out to almost exactly 4, confirming that each time
% the step size h is halved, the trapezoidal rule's error shrinks to
% about a quarter of its previous value -- the hallmark of an error term
% that scales as O(h^2).

% a) Define the concentration model and compute the true AUC
C = @(t) 10*(exp(-0.5*t) - exp(-2*t));
F = @(t) 10*(-2*exp(-0.5*t) + 0.5*exp(-2*t));
trueAUC = F(8) - F(0);
disp(trueAUC)

% b) Sparse and dense sampling schedules
tSparse = 0:2:8;
cSparse = C(tSparse);
AUCsparse = trapz(tSparse, cSparse);

tDense = 0:0.25:8;
cDense = C(tDense);
AUCdense = trapz(tDense, cDense);

% c) Signed errors
errSparse = AUCsparse - trueAUC;
errDense = AUCdense - trueAUC;
fprintf('errSparse = %.4f, errDense = %.4f\n', errSparse, errDense);

% d) Locate the peak
tFine = linspace(0, 8, 1000);
cFine = C(tFine);
[peakC, idx] = max(cFine);
peakTime = tFine(idx);
fprintf('Peak concentration %.4f mg/L occurs near t = %.4f h\n', peakC, peakTime);

% e) Written explanation:
% The sparse schedule samples only at t = 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 hours, but the
% true concentration peaks very early, near t ~ 0.9 hours, and has
% already dropped substantially by t = 2. Since trapz only ever draws
% straight lines between the points it's given, and the sparse schedule
% has no point anywhere near the peak, the straight line from (0, C(0))
% to (2, C(2)) badly underestimates the true area under the sharp early
% rise-and-fall the real curve traces out.

% f) Written comment:
% Rather than uniformly resampling everything every 15 minutes, a
% clinical study could use a NON-uniform schedule: sample frequently
% (e.g., every 15-30 minutes) during the first couple of hours, when the
% concentration is changing quickly, then switch to the coarser 2-hour
% spacing once the curve has flattened out and is decaying slowly --
% this concentrates the limited number of blood draws where the curve's
% shape actually needs them most, without requiring dense sampling for
% the whole 8-hour window.

% a) Define f and sample at 9 equally spaced nodes
f = @(x) 1./(1 + 25*x.^2);
xnodes = linspace(-1, 1, 9);
ynodes = f(xnodes);

% b) Exact degree-8 fit
p8 = polyfit(xnodes, ynodes, 8);
xf = linspace(-1, 1, 400);
y8 = polyval(p8, xf);

% c) Much lower-degree fit (degree 2, not exact)
p2 = polyfit(xnodes, ynodes, 2);
y2 = polyval(p2, xf);

% d) Maximum deviation from the true function
yTrue = f(xf);
maxErr8 = max(abs(y8 - yTrue));
maxErr2 = max(abs(y2 - yTrue));
fprintf('maxErr8 = %.4f, maxErr2 = %.4f\n', maxErr8, maxErr2);

% e) Compare all three near the edge of the domain
trueAt09 = f(0.9);
deg8At09 = polyval(p8, 0.9);
deg2At09 = polyval(p2, 0.9);
fprintf('true = %.4f, degree-8 fit = %.4f, degree-2 fit = %.4f\n', trueAt09, deg8At09, deg2At09);

% f) Written explanation:
% At x=0.9, the true function is a small positive number (~0.047), and
% the crude degree-2 fit stays reasonably close (~0.031). But the exact
% degree-8 fit -- despite passing through all 9 original nodes exactly
% -- swings down to roughly -0.96, wildly overshooting in the wrong
% direction near the edge of the domain. This is Runge's phenomenon: a
% high-degree polynomial forced to interpolate equally spaced points can
% oscillate with increasing amplitude near the edges of the interval,
% even while matching the data at the nodes perfectly. The general rule:
% do not trust a high-degree polynomial interpolant's behavior BETWEEN
% or near the EDGES of equally spaced nodes just because it passes
% through the nodes themselves -- for many points, a piecewise method
% (like a spline or a lower-degree local fit) is safer than one single
% high-degree polynomial.

starterCode:
% Problem: From Data Points to Area: Does the Interpolant Change the Answer?
% Name:
% Date:

clear; clc;

% a) Build the exact Lagrange cubic through the 4 points using poly()/conv()
pH = [5 6 7 8];
activity = [20 55 78 40];


% b) AUC_raw: trapz directly on the 4 raw points


% c) AUC_poly: trapz on a fine evaluation of the Lagrange cubic;
%    AUC_exact: using polyint/polyval; confirm they agree


% d) Display AUC_raw and AUC_poly; compute their difference


% e) Written explanation: why do AUC_raw and AUC_poly disagree?


% f) Written comment: a situation where trusting AUC_poly could backfire

solutionCode:
% a) Build the exact Lagrange cubic in coefficient form
pH = [5 6 7 8];
activity = [20 55 78 40];

LagrangeCoeffs = 0;
for ii = 1:length(pH)
    L = 1;
    for jj = 1:length(pH)
        if jj ~= ii
            c = poly(pH(jj));
            c_scaled = c / (pH(ii) - pH(jj));
            L = conv(L, c_scaled);
        end
    end
    term = L * activity(ii);
    LagrangeCoeffs = LagrangeCoeffs + term;
end
disp(polyval(LagrangeCoeffs, pH))   % should match activity

% b) Raw trapz estimate on the 4 original points
AUC_raw = trapz(pH, activity);

% c) Trapz on a fine evaluation of the interpolant, and the exact value
xf = linspace(pH(1), pH(end), 2000);
yf = polyval(LagrangeCoeffs, xf);
AUC_poly = trapz(xf, yf);

polyIntCoeffs = polyint(LagrangeCoeffs);
AUC_exact = diff(polyval(polyIntCoeffs, [pH(1) pH(end)]));
disp(abs(AUC_poly - AUC_exact) < 1e-3)

% d) Compare
fprintf('AUC_raw = %.4f, AUC_poly = %.4f\n', AUC_raw, AUC_poly);
diffAUC = AUC_poly - AUC_raw;
disp(diffAUC)

% e) Written explanation:
% AUC_raw and AUC_poly disagree because trapz on just the 4 raw points
% assumes the activity changes in a perfectly straight line BETWEEN each
% pair of measured pH values, while the Lagrange cubic instead captures
% the actual curved, bell-shaped rise-and-fall that a single straight
% line between just two points cannot represent. Since the true
% biological relationship almost certainly curves smoothly rather than
% forming sharp straight-line corners at each measured pH, the
% interpolant-based estimate captures area near the peak that the raw
% trapezoidal segments miss, which is why AUC_poly ends up larger.

% f) Written comment:
% Trusting AUC_poly could backfire if the true activity-vs-pH
% relationship is NOT well-approximated by a single low-order polynomial
% through so few points -- with only 4 points, the cubic is forced
% through all of them exactly, but (as with Runge's phenomenon) that
% same exact fit could just as easily overshoot or oscillate between
% points instead of smoothly filling in the true curve, especially if
% the real relationship has a sharper peak or an asymmetric shape the
% four sparse samples don't fully reveal -- more data points, not a
% fancier interpolant over the same 4 points, would be the safer fix.
